  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">PLASTICS AS KEY LIMITING FACTOR FOR SHOR</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Plastic debris in marine environments is increasing concern because of its persistence and its negative effects on a wide range of marine taxa. Discarded plastics account for 73 percent of marine debris globally, affecting many aquatic species through entanglement and ingestion of small fragments (i.e., microplastics). There is evidence that microplastic ingestion can reduce feeding rates, cause inflammatory responses, and reduce growth rate and fertility, among other negative effects in laboratory experiments. However, current knowledge of the negative effects on wildlife is largely based on post-mortem examinations. Sublethal effects are poorly documented, and consequently underestimated. CORBIDI proposes to determine the cost of microplastic load in a group of long-distance migratory shorebirds and relate this to their migratory performance before northward migration. The presence of microplastics will be evaluated by capturing live birds and collecting fecal samples in major staging and non-breeding sites in the southern hemisphere. Each bird&apos;s body condition will be examined using performance measures indices, and blood chemistry parameters will be measured for Red Knots as an additional measure of bird health. Understanding the consequences of microplastic load in long-distance migratory shorebirds is crucial to determining population vulnerability during the annual life cycle. Additionally, CORBIDI will build local capacity in shorebird capture and identification techniques and raise awareness against plastic use in local communities throughout directed workshops.</narrative>
  </description>
